            <div xml:id="d623">

                <pb facs="0657" n="559" sameAs="frus:frus1930v02/pg559"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.711/49: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The President of the United Press</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Bickel</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Acting Secretary of
                            State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >New York</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1930-02-12">February 12, 1930</date>.<lb/>[Received 5:39
                        p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>For over three months now the <hi rendition="#italic">Shanghai Evening Post</hi>,
                    most important American daily in China, has suffered under a postal ban applied
                    by Shanghai authorities because of <hi rendition="#italic">Post</hi> insistence
                    that laws protecting property <hi rendition="#italic">Evening Post</hi> be
                    impartially enforced. The imposition of the postal ban bears every indication of
                    being done in effort to embarrass <hi rendition="#italic">Post</hi> and
                    indirectly control its editorial utterances. As postal ban against <hi
                        rendition="#italic">North China Daily News</hi>, leading British paper which
                    previously fell under displeasure Chinese authorities, only enforced about two
                    months there are evidences discrimination against <hi rendition="#italic">Post.
                        Shanghai Evening Post</hi>, a United Press client paper, is owned <pb
                        facs="0658" n="560" xml:id="pg560"/> by C. V. Starr, a leading American
                    businessman in Shanghai now in New York, edited by Carl Crow, well-known
                    American newspaper man in China. <hi rendition="#italic">Evening Post</hi> been
                    factor utmost importance to United States, deserves any support you or Minister
                    Johnson can give them in their fight to have the <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Post</hi> handled nondiscriminatory basis by Chinese post office. Above is
                    for your information and any assistance that you can properly render.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Karl A. Bickel</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
